 "brief": "Spectral networks — We introduce new geometric objects called spectral networks. Spectral net- works are networks of trajectories on Riemann surfaces obeying certain local rules.",
 "extended": "**Spectral networks**\n\n*Davide Gaiotto, 1 Gregory W. Moore 2 and Andrew Neitzke 3*\n\n87 pp, 51 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** We introduce new geometric objects called spectral networks. Spectral net- works are networks of trajectories on Riemann surfaces obeying certain local rules. Spec- tral networks arise naturally in four-dimensionalN = 2 theories coupled to surface defects, particularly the theories of class S. In these theories spectral networks provide a useful tool for the computation of BPS degeneracies: the network directly determines the degen- eracies of solitons living on the surface defect, which in turn determine the degeneracies for particles living in the 4d bulk. Spectral networks also lead to a new map between ﬂat GL(K, C) connections on a two-dimensional surface C and ﬂat abelian connections on an appropriate branched cover Σ of C. This construction produces natural coordinate sys- tems on moduli spaces of ﬂat GL(K, C) connections on C, which we conjecture are cluster coordinate systems.\n\n**Outline.** 1 Introduction and summary; 2 A brief review of theories of class S; 3 Geometric description of BPS states; 3.1 4d BPS states; 3.2 Solitons; 3.3 Framed 2d-4d BPS states; 3.4 Enhanced degeneracies; 3.5 A problem of signs; 4 Basics of framed 2d-4d indices; 4.1 Generating functions of framed 2d-4d indic; 4.2 Formal products and composition; 4.3 The spectral network W; 5.1 Walls as trajectories; 5.2 Joints and wall-crossing for; 5.6 Homotopy invariance; 6 Varying; 6.1 Endpoints crossing S-walls; 6.2 K-walls and the degenerate spectral networ\n\n**Extracted claims (proposals, not verified).**\n- Theorem: F(·,W) descends to a homomorphism",
